This question comes up on every Angular 22 project I work on. Someone asks whether to use Signals or RxJS. The answer is almost always “both — for different things.” Here’s the practical breakdown.
The Short Answer Nobody Gives
Signals for component state and derived values. RxJS for complex async streams, real-time data, and operations that benefit from the observable operator chain.
Both coexist in Angular 22. They’re not competing. They solve different problems. The mistake is using one for everything.
What Signals Are Good At
Signals shine for synchronous state that components read and update. Theme toggle. Sidebar collapse. Filter selections. Loading states. Form values. Pagination page number. Selected rows in a table.
export class UserTableComponent {
// All of these are perfect for Signals
currentPage = signal(1)
pageSize = signal(20)
searchQuery = signal('')
selectedRows = signal<Set<number>>(new Set())
sortColumn = signal<keyof User>('name')
sortDirection = signal<'asc' | 'desc'>('asc')
isLoading = signal(false)
// Derived state - computed automatically when dependencies change
hasSelection = computed(() => this.selectedRows().size > 0)
selectionCount = computed(() => this.selectedRows().size)
isFirstPage = computed(() => this.currentPage() === 1)
// httpResource for async data - built on Signals
users = httpResource<PaginatedResponse<User>>(() => ({
url: '/api/users',
params: {
page: this.currentPage(),
pageSize: this.pageSize(),
search: this.searchQuery()
}
}))
// Computed from async resource
userList = computed(() => this.users.value()?.data ?? [])
totalPages = computed(() =>
Math.ceil((this.users.value()?.total ?? 0) / this.pageSize())
)
}Everything here is synchronous state or derived from async data through httpResource. No subscriptions. No unsubscribe. No destroy logic. The template reads signal values and re-renders only when they change.
What RxJS Is Still Better At
Three scenarios where RxJS is genuinely better than Signals in 2026.
Debounced search input:
export class SearchComponent implements OnDestroy {
private searchInput = new Subject<string>()
private destroy$ = new Subject<void>()
searchResults = signal<SearchResult[]>([])
isSearching = signal(false) constructor(private searchService: SearchService) {
this.searchInput.pipe(
debounceTime(300),
distinctUntilChanged(),
filter(query => query.length >= 2),
tap(() => this.isSearching.set(true)),
switchMap(query => this.searchService.search(query)),
takeUntil(this.destroy$)
).subscribe({
next: results => {
this.searchResults.set(results)
this.isSearching.set(false)
},
error: () => this.isSearching.set(false)
})
}
onSearchInput(query: string) {
this.searchInput.next(query)
}
ngOnDestroy() {
this.destroy$.next()
this.destroy$.complete()
}
}
The debounce, distinctUntilChanged, filter, and switchMap chain is cleaner in RxJS than the Signal equivalent. Signals don’t have built-in debouncing. You’d need a debounced() helper or a setTimeout-based approach that's messier than the RxJS chain.
WebSocket real-time data:
typescript
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class RealtimeService {
private socket$ = webSocket<DashboardUpdate>('wss://api.example.com/ws') // RxJS for the stream, Signal for the current value
private _metrics = signal<DashboardMetrics | null>(null)
metrics = this._metrics.asReadonly()
constructor() {
this.socket$.pipe(
filter(update => update.type === 'metrics'),
map(update => update.data as DashboardMetrics),
retry({ count: 3, delay: 2000 }),
share()
).subscribe(metrics => this._metrics.set(metrics))
}
}
The WebSocket stream with retry logic and sharing is natural RxJS. The current value exposed as a Signal so components can read it reactively. RxJS for the stream management. Signal for the current state.
Multiple HTTP requests combined:
// When you need to combine multiple async sources with complex logic
combineLatest([
this.userService.currentUser$,
this.permissionsService.permissions$,
this.featureFlags.flags$
]).pipe(
map(([user, permissions, flags]) => ({
canEdit: permissions.includes('edit') && flags.editEnabled,
canDelete: permissions.includes('delete') && user.role === 'admin',
canExport: flags.exportEnabled
})),
takeUntilDestroyed()
).subscribe(access => this.accessControl.set(access))Three streams combined with transformation logic. combineLatest handles this cleanly. The result goes into a Signal for component consumption.
The Pattern That Works Best
RxJS for the stream. Signal for the current value components read.
This pattern appears throughout the codebase:
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class NotificationsService {
private _count = signal(0)
private _notifications = signal<Notification[]>([])
count = this._count.asReadonly()
notifications = this._notifications.asReadonly() constructor(private http: HttpClient) {
// RxJS for polling every 30 seconds
interval(30000).pipe(
startWith(0),
switchMap(() => this.http.get<Notification[]>('/api/notifications')),
takeUntilDestroyed()
).subscribe(notifications => {
this._notifications.set(notifications)
this._count.set(notifications.filter(n => !n.read).length)
})
}
markAsRead(id: number) {
// Signal update for immediate UI response
this._notifications.update(notifications =>
notifications.map(n => n.id === id ? { ...n, read: true } : n)
)
this._count.update(count => Math.max(0, count - 1))
// HTTP call in background
this.http.patch(`/api/notifications/${id}`, { read: true }).subscribe()
}
}
Polling interval managed with RxJS. Current state exposed as Signals. UI updates immediately on markAsRead via Signal mutation. HTTP call happens in background.
When to Use toSignal and toObservable
Angular provides bridge utilities for converting between Signals and Observables when you need to use them together.
import { toSignal, toObservable } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop'// Convert Observable to Signal — useful when third-party returns Observable
const routeParam = toSignal(this.route.paramMap.pipe(
map(params => params.get('id'))
))
// Convert Signal to Observable — useful for RxJS operators
const searchQuery$ = toObservable(this.searchQuery)
searchQuery$.pipe(
debounceTime(300),
distinctUntilChanged(),
switchMap(query => this.search(query))
).subscribe(results => this.results.set(results))
toSignal wraps an Observable in a Signal — the Signal updates whenever the Observable emits. toObservable converts a Signal to an Observable — emits whenever the Signal changes.
These bridges let you use Signals and RxJS together without picking one over the other.
The Simple Rule
If you’re writing new BehaviorSubject for component state in Angular 22 — use a Signal instead. If you're writing signal() for a debounced search or a WebSocket stream — use RxJS instead. If you need both in the same service — use both, bridge with toSignal and toObservable where needed.
